# Visibility & Data Binding

## Visibility is a render-time predicate, never a tree mutation

- `Block.visibility` (author/device/locale intent) and `Block.visibleWhen`
  (runtime predicate against `renderContext`) are both evaluated by
  `visibilityResolve` inside `RenderPage` — identically for canvas
  (with ghost affordance), preview, and Open Page (full omission).
- Hiding a block NEVER removes its `children`, props, or any other data.
  Delete is a distinct, explicit tree mutation (`blockTree.remove`) — do
  not conflate the two anywhere in the code.
- Hidden blocks remain visible in the Outline Tree, shown dimmed. Do not
  filter them out of the outline.

## Evaluation order (centralized in visibilityResolve.ts — don't duplicate)

```
1. hiddenOnPublish && surface !== 'canvas'  → hide
2. hiddenOnCanvas && surface === 'canvas'   → ghost
3. device in hiddenDevices                  → hide/ghost by surface
4. locale in hiddenLocales                  → hide/ghost by surface
5. visibleWhen fails                        → hide/ghost by surface
6. else                                     → show
```
Blocks must never special-case this logic themselves — call
`visibilityResolve`, don't reimplement it.

## Item-scoped context (§23.2.1) — the leak to watch for

- Inside a repeater's `itemTemplate`, predicates evaluate against
  `{ ...renderContext, item: <current bound item> }` — but this merged
  context is scoped ONLY to that one expansion pass.
- Outside a repeater, any predicate referencing `item.*` must evaluate to
  `false` (fail safe / hidden) — never `undefined`-crash, never leak an
  `item` from a sibling repeater's expansion.
- `visibilityResolve` itself does not construct the merged context — it
  only reads whatever `renderContext` object it's given. The merging
  happens in `dataBinding.ts` during expansion. Keep that separation; don't
  have `visibilityResolve` reach into binding internals.

## Data binding (§25) — repeater + DataSource, not monolithic widgets

- CMS loops (e.g. "blog cards") are built as: `repeater` block with
  `dataBinding: { sourceId, params, itemTemplate: Block[] }`, where
  `itemTemplate` is an ordinary primitive tree (`box` > `image` > `heading`
  > `text` > `button`), never a locked composite type.
- Binding tokens (`{{item.title}}`, `{{item.image}}`) resolve to strings/
  URLs typed by the DataSource's `itemSchema`. Unknown tokens resolve to
  empty string — never leak a raw `{{...}}` into an XSS-sensitive sink.
- Any HTML-bearing item field still passes through `sanitizeRichText`
  before injection — token resolution does not bypass sanitization.
- Support two fetch strategies and keep them in parity:
  - **A — host-resolved** (SSR/RSC on Open Page): host resolves items,
    engine only expands.
  - **B — client callback**: engine calls `fetchDataSource(sourceId,
    params)` (never imports host `services/` directly).
  - Both must produce identical DOM (same item order, same token
    resolution, same loading/empty/error state markup) for the same
    DataSource + params — write a parity test against one fixture
    DataSource covering both strategies.
- Loading / empty / error states use structural attributes only (e.g.
  `data-binding-state="empty"`) — styling is still 100% author CSS, the
  engine does not bake in visual states.
- Attaching/activating `dataBinding` on any block is gated by host
  capability `allowDataBinding`. If ungated `dataBinding` is present in
  loaded JSON (e.g. copied from another page), render it INERT — template
  only, no fetch — never crash and never silently query.

## Required tests for this area

- Visibility parity across canvas (ghost)/preview (omit)/Open Page (omit).
- Item-scoped `item.*` predicate fails safe outside a repeater.
- Repeater expands `itemTemplate` once per item; empty/loading/error states
  match across both fetch strategies.
- Binding tokens never emit unescaped HTML from item data.
